Exactly. And there’s a profound distinction between being peaceful and being harmless.

Jesus taught people to turn the other cheek. But he also overturned the money changers’ tables in the temple.

He showed compassion to the vulnerable, challenged the powerful, and refused to confuse goodness with obedience.

And I like your carpenter metaphor because a hammer has more than one purpose.

A hammer can build a home, repair what’s broken, drive a nail, or tear down something rotten.

The wisdom is knowing which job you’re doing.

There’s a difference between vengeance and justice. Between aggression and conviction. Between having the power to hurt and having the discipline to protect.

Meekness is not weakness. Mercy is not surrender. And peace is not always the absence of confrontation.

Perhaps the highest expression of strength isn’t bringing down the hammer.

It’s knowing when to pick it up—and when to put it down.